course: everyday
lesson: 226
title: Compliments and respecting differences
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A compliment is a small gift of words. When you get one, the polite thing is to take it with a simple «Thank you», not to argue with it and not to boast about it.
explain: The best compliments are about things people choose or do: a drawing, a goal, their kindness, their effort, a tasty meal. Comments about someone's body or weight can make them uncomfortable, even when meant kindly.
explain: People are different in many ways: accents, religions and clothing, festivals, families, food, skin colour, size and faces. Different is not wrong, and everyone deserves to be treated kindly and fairly.
explain: Example: a new boy in Glasgow speaks English with a Polish accent and does not know that you queue for lunch. His classmates listen patiently, show him how lunch works and ask about the festivals his family keeps, and soon he feels at home.
fact: When someone compliments you, say «Thank you» instead of disagreeing.
fact: After a compliment, say thank you and leave it there, because boasting spoils the good feeling.
fact: When a friend does well, say «Well done!» even if you did not do so well yourself.
fact: Compliment things people choose, like their drawing, kindness or effort, rather than their body or weight.
fact: Telling a cook the meal was delicious lets them know their work was enjoyed.
fact: A kind, specific compliment can help a shy person feel more confident.
fact: Learning another language is hard, and many people speak two or more, so never copy or tease an accent.
fact: Religious clothing like a headscarf, turban or kippah is important to the people who wear it, so treat it as normal.
fact: Festivals like Diwali, Eid, Hanukkah and Lunar New Year are interesting to learn about, and wishing people well is friendly.
fact: Families come in many shapes: two mums, two dads, one parent or grandparents, and all deserve respect.
fact: If someone makes a racist or mean joke, do not laugh; say it is not okay or tell an adult.
fact: People have the right to their own beliefs, so do not mock or argue about someone's religion.
fact: People are vegetarian or vegan for health, belief or love of animals, so never try to make them eat meat.
fact: A child new to the country is still learning the customs, so explain kindly how things work.
exercise: Your teacher says «What a lovely story you wrote!» What is the best reply? | «Thank you!» | «No, it's rubbish really» | «I know, it's the best in the class» | «You only say that to everyone»
exercise: Why can saying «No, it isn't good» to a compliment be a problem? | It can make the person who gave it feel awkward | It shows you are very modest and wise | It makes the compliment more true | It is the polite way to answer any compliment in English
exercise: Your friend scores the winning goal, but you missed every shot. What does a good friend say? | «Well done, that was a great goal!» | «You were just lucky» | «I would have scored if I had the ball» | Nothing, and walk off
exercise: Which compliment is kindest and safest? | «I love how you used colours in your painting» | «You look so much thinner than you did last year» | «You'd be prettier if you were taller» | «Your face is a funny shape but nice»
exercise: Grandma cooked a roast dinner you really enjoyed. What should you do? | Tell her it was delicious | Say nothing, since cooking is her job | Ask why she did not make pizza | Tell her it was nearly as good as the café
exercise: A shy classmate drew a beautiful horse. How could you help her feel confident? | Tell her exactly what you like about it | Ask her to draw one for you instead, since she's so good | Say it is good for a shy person | Show the class without asking her
exercise: Someone says your hair looks great, and you start telling everyone how you are the best-looking in class. What went wrong? | Boasting spoiled the good feeling of the compliment | Nothing, because compliments must be shared with everyone | You should have said your hair looks bad | You should have given a compliment back first
exercise: A new classmate speaks English with a strong accent. What is right? | Listen patiently and never copy or tease the accent | Copy the accent to make everyone laugh | Ask him to speak only once he has lost the accent completely | Answer him very loudly and slowly every time
exercise: A girl in your class wears a headscarf. How should you treat it? | As normal, and ask politely only if you want to learn | Ask her to take it off for PE photos | Point it out to the class as unusual | Tell her it does not belong at school
exercise: Your friend celebrates Diwali. What is a friendly thing to do? | Show interest and wish him a happy Diwali | Say your own festivals are better | Ignore it, as it is not your festival | Tell him festivals are only for little children
exercise: A classmate lives with her two dads. What is the right attitude? | Her family is just as normal as yours | It is fine to ask her where her real mum is | Her family is strange and worth talking about | It is kind to feel sorry for her all day
exercise: A new boy is much taller than everyone and has a scar on his face. What should you do? | Treat him kindly and never stare or point | Ask him loudly in front of everyone what happened to his face | Give him a nickname about his height | Keep away in case he is unfriendly
exercise: A boy tells a racist joke at lunch and others laugh. What should you do? | Not laugh, and say it is not okay or tell an adult | Laugh quietly along with the others so that you fit in | Tell an even funnier joke to change the subject | Laugh, because jokes are never meant to hurt
exercise: Your friend believes something different from you about God. What is right? | Respect her belief and do not mock it | Argue until she agrees with you | Tell her she is stupid for believing it | Stop being her friend
exercise: A vegan friend comes to your birthday party. What is kind? | Make sure there is food she can eat and never push meat on her | Hide meat in her food so she gets protein | Tell her one sausage will not hurt | Tease her until she tries some ham
exercise: A girl who has just moved from India does not know that pupils line up before class. What should you do? | Explain kindly how it works here | Laugh when she gets it wrong | Report her to the teacher for being rude | Leave her to work it out herself
test: A boy in Melbourne gets told his model ship is amazing. Which reply is best? | «Thanks, I worked hard on it» | «It's terrible, the sail is wrong» | «Yes, mine is always the best» | «I don't believe you mean that»
test: Two friends ran a race and Mia won, while Lily came last. What shows Lily is a good friend? | She says «Well done, Mia!» and means it | She says Mia started early, before the whistle went | She stops talking to Mia for the day | She tells everyone the race was unfair
test: Which compliment might make someone uncomfortable even if it is meant kindly? | «You've lost loads of weight» | «That was a really clever answer» | «You were so kind to help Sam» | «I love your story about the dragon»
test: A new girl from Ukraine speaks three languages but makes small mistakes in English. Which is true? | Speaking several languages is hard work and deserves respect | Her mistakes show she is not clever | Copying her mistakes would help her learn | She should only speak once she is perfect
test: A Sikh boy in your class wears a turban, and you are curious. What is a good way to learn more? | Ask him politely, if he is happy to talk about it | Pull it gently to see how it is tied | Ask the whole class to guess what is under it, as a game | Tell him it looks like a towel
test: At lunch someone says «Her family is weird, she only has a grandma». What is the fair reply? | Families come in many shapes, and all deserve respect | Agree, so that you do not get teased about it too | Ask the girl to explain her family to everyone at the table | Say nothing, as it is just a fact
test: Your friends laugh at a joke that makes fun of people from another country. Why should you not laugh along? | Laughing encourages it, and speaking up helps those hurt | Jokes about countries are always true | You would get a much better laugh by telling your own joke instead | It is fine as long as nobody from that country hears
test: A boy who has just come from Syria does not know how the school lunch line works. What helps most? | Showing him kindly what to do | Leaving him so he learns faster | Telling him he should have known | Asking the teacher to move him to another class
test: A friend refuses chicken at your house because her family does not eat meat. What should you do? | Respect it and offer her something else | Tell her she is just being fussy and should eat it | Say that chicken is not really meat | Make her try a bite to be polite
test: teacher said my drawing was amazing, i said nah its rubbish. was that ok | No, just say thank you | Yes, saying no is polite | Yes, always argue with praise | No, you should say yours is the best
test: new kid talks with an accent, some boys copying him lol. should i join in | No, never copy or tease an accent | Yes, if he laughs too | Yes, it's only a joke | Only when the teacher isn't there
test: my mate's vegan, can i sneak some ham in her sandwich as a joke | No, never trick her into eating meat | Yes, a little bit of ham won't matter to her | Yes, if you tell her afterwards | Only if she is very hungry
test: everyone's laughing at a mean joke about someone's skin colour. what do i do | Don't laugh, say it's not okay or tell an adult | Laugh so you fit in | Tell an even meaner joke | Walk off and say nothing ever
explain: A boy in Arizona made fun of a classmate's hijab (a Muslim headscarf) every day; the girl became afraid to come to school, and the school treated it as bullying and the boy was suspended.
explain: A girl in Edinburgh told a new boy his lunch "stinks"; others joined in, he began skipping lunch, and he lost weight before a teacher noticed.
fact: Mocking someone's religion, skin, body, family or food is bullying, and schools take it seriously.
fact: Children who are mocked for being different can stop eating, stop learning and become afraid to go to school.
test: new kid talks funny, can we copy his accent | No, mocking him is bullying and hurts | Yes, it's just a laugh | Yes, he'll get used to it | Only behind his back
test: Why did the new boy start skipping lunch? | He was being mocked for his food | He just wasn't hungry at lunchtime any more | Lunch was too early | He wanted to lose weight
test: they keep laughing at my friend's wheelchair | Stand with your friend and tell an adult | Laugh along so they stop | Walk away and say nothing | Laugh at them instead
test: did u hear some boys made a racist joke about the new kid and everyone laughed and he went home crying? real lesson? | Don't laugh; say it's not okay or tell an adult | Laugh so you don't stand out | It's fine if he didn't hear it | Only adults can stop it
test: apparently the new girl doesn't eat meat cos her family's in a weird cult, someone said. what's probably just a rumour? | The cult; people are vegetarian for health, belief or animals | That she doesn't eat meat | That vegetarians eat vegetables | That families have customs
test: my mate says that kid with two mums gets teased so much he's moving schools. pass it on? | No, it's gossip; all families deserve respect | Yes, everyone should know | Yes, so the teachers find out | Yes, if you say it kindly
exam: Identify the appropriate response upon receiving a compliment. | To say «Thank you» | To disagree with the compliment | To boast further of one's success | To return a larger compliment at once
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises religious clothing such as a headscarf, turban or kippah? | It is important to its wearer and is to be treated as normal | It is a costume worn only on festivals | It may be removed by others as a joke | It is worn only by adults
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice where another person makes a racist joke. | One does not laugh and says it is not acceptable or tells an adult | One laughs so as not to offend the speaker | One repeats the joke to others | One remains silent and laughs later
exam: Ascertain the subjects that are most appropriate for compliments. | Things a person chooses, such as effort, kindness or drawings | A person's body or weight | A person's height or skin | A person's wealth and possessions
